# Design system — Bogenschuss Logbuch (Sleek Paperstyle)

This is the agent-facing description of the logandaim.at visual identity. It is a rulebook, not a
history: follow it literally when building UI. Values live in
[`frontend/src/app/globals.css`](../../frontend/src/app/globals.css) and nowhere else. This file
names tokens and states rules; it never restates hex codes. The product-level intent and the
decision log live in [`specs/product/visual-identity.md`](../../specs/product/visual-identity.md).

## The identity

**Bogenschuss Logbuch — "der Wald auf Papier, die Effizienz im Browser."** The app joins the haptics
of a handwritten hunting logbook to the speed and precision of a web app. It is not a game: it is a
practical, fast tool for archers and hunters in the field or at the desk.

- **Skeuomorphism, but restrained.** Flat, light paper surfaces, fine lines, an organic feel, and
  a lot of room to breathe. No leather textures, no 3D book spines, no ornament.
- **Utility first.** Every interaction is fast, accessible, and logical. This is a data-capture app,
  not a lifestyle magazine.
- **Calm focus.** The interface must not distract on the range. Natural, unobtrusive contrast keeps
  it readable outdoors.

## Tokens

The single source is the block in `frontend/src/app/globals.css`. Every token below is defined for
light and dark. Add a token only together with an entry here.

| Token | Meaning | Use for |
|----|----|----|
| `--background` | The desk the paper lies on | App shell and page bodies |
| `--foreground` | Warm ink | Body text, headings, default icon colour |
| `--surface` | A sheet of paper | Cards, tables, dialogs, header, sidebar |
| `--surface-muted` | A second sheet / greyed paper | Table headers, hover rows, skeleton blocks, tab strips, inactive tracks |
| `--border` | Pencil hairline | Borders, dividers, table rules |
| `--contour` | Faint grid line | Tick rules (`rule-ticks`), scale marks, quiet sheet rules |
| `--primary` | **The one brand colour: hunter green** | One primary button per screen, active nav item, the active step |
| `--primary-foreground` | Paper on hunter green | Button labels and icons drawn on `--primary` |
| `--secondary` | Neutral paper fill | Inactive tracks, muted chips, segmented-control rest state |
| `--muted-foreground` | Secondary ink | Hints, captions, table headers, metadata |
| `--destructive` | Failure, miss, impact point | Destructive buttons, inline validation, error stamps |
| `--success` | Confirmed outcome ("gespeichert") | Success stamps and alerts |
| `--warning` | Attention without failure | Pending/edit states, moderate hits, touch-target debug outline |
| `--ring` | Focus indicator | Every `focus-visible` outline |
| `--radius` | Corner radius | `--radius-sm` through `--radius-xl` are derived from it |
| `--shadow-paper` | Sheet shadow | The `paper` class. Soft in light, `none` in dark |
| `--shadow-overlay` | Floating-sheet shadow | The `paper-overlay` class: dialogs, menus, tooltips |
| `--font-sans` | Interface face: **Inter** | Menus, tables, forms, small figures. Never name a family in a component |
| `--font-serif` | Book face: **Lora** | Titles only — page, card, and dialog headings |
| `--font-mono` | Machine values only | References, base URLs, token names, file names. Never for prose |

Inter is the work face and Lora is the book face. Headings use `font-serif`; everything the user
works with uses `font-sans`. Weights are listed in `layout.tsx` — add one there before using a
`font-*` weight that is not loaded, or the browser will synthesise it. Geist Mono is the machine
face.

Dark mode is class-based (`@custom-variant dark`), with `prefers-color-scheme` as the default. The
gallery sets `.light` and `.dark` on wrappers to show both themes side by side.

## Colour discipline

There is exactly **one brand colour**. Everything else is paper, ink, or an explicit meaning:

- Hunter green: brand, primary action, active state. Nothing else.
- Paper and warm neutrals: all structure.
- Red, amber/ochre, success green: meaning only, never decoration. Always paired with a word.

## Paper language

Six elements carry the identity. Use them; do not invent a seventh.

1. **Papier (the sheet).** Content sits on `Card` — a hairline and a whisper of shadow, never a heavy
   border or an elevation card. In dark mode the shadow is `none` and the hairline alone separates
   the sheet.
2. **Titel (serif headings).** Page, card, and dialog titles are `font-serif`. The serif is the
   logbook's hand and the only thing that carries the historical character; body text never uses it.
3. **Ruled line (the divider).** `Separator` draws `rule-ticks`: small ticks along a hairline, like
   the printed ruling of a logbook column. Sections are divided by a ruled line, never by empty
   space alone or by a shadow.
4. **Feine Kontur (hairline controls).** Inputs and selects are flat with a 1px `--border` contour
   that turns `--primary` on focus and darkens slightly on hover.
5. **Stempel (stamps).** `Badge` is a rubber stamp: hairline box, tracked small caps, a tint of the
   semantic colour. Status always carries its word.
6. **Millimetergrid (grid data).** Charts, targets, and statistics are thin geometric lines on a
   faint grid, like graph paper — drawn from `--contour`, never filled shapes or gradients.

## Rules

1. **Semantic utilities only.** Use `bg-surface`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`,
   `bg-primary`, `text-destructive`, and so on. Never write a raw palette class (`bg-stone-100`,
   `text-green-800`, `border-red-200`) or a hex value in a component. `check:design` fails the
   build otherwise. Paper patterns come from `rule-ticks`, `rule-ticks-quiet`, `leader`,
   `select-chevron`, or `checkbox-tick`; their SVG masks live in `globals.css` so they inherit
   `currentColor` instead of hardcoding a stroke colour. An inline style may reference only
   `var(--…)` tokens.
2. **Contrast is a gate.** Text on a background meets WCAG 2.2 AA (4.5:1 normal text). The gallery
   measures every documented pair; a change that drops a pair below AA is not accepted. The amber
   from the original guide fails as text on paper, so `--warning` is its darker sibling.
3. **Colour is never the only signal.** `Wertungszone`, status, and validation carry their meaning
   in text or an icon too. Badges and alerts always include the word.
4. **Touch targets are at least 44 by 44 pixels** on anything an archer uses with gloves: buttons,
   nav items, checkbox and radio rows, slider thumbs. The target and the drawn control are not the
   same thing: a control keeps its neighbour's height and extends its hit area with a `before`
   overlay. Never make a control taller than its row to reach 44px.
5. **Focus is always visible.** Interactive elements use `focus-visible:outline-2` with
   `outline-ring`. Never remove an outline without an equally visible replacement.
6. **One primary action per screen.** Everything else is `secondary` or `ghost`. Destructive
   actions use `destructive` and are never the primary button.
7. **German UI copy, English code.** Labels follow the ubiquitous language (`Anlage`, `Parcours`,
   `Ziel`, `Pflock`, `Runde`, `Schuss`, `Wertung`). Do not hardcode new copy in a component beyond
   the gallery examples.
8. **Spacing scale** is the Tailwind default (4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32 px). Page bodies use `p-6`,
   cards use `p-6`, form fields stack with `space-y-1.5` inside `Field`. White space is part of the
   identity: prefer a wider gap to another rule.
9. **Variants come from `cva`.** New variants are added in the component's `cva` definition, not by
   passing arbitrary classes at call sites. Public variant names stay backwards compatible.
10. **Flat by default.** Edges come from `border-border` and `--contour`. Cards carry the `paper`
    shadow class, which is `none` in dark mode; only overlays that genuinely float (dialogs,
    dropdowns, tooltips) carry `paper-overlay`. No hover elevation, gradients, or glass.
11. **Angular paper, not pills.** `--radius` is 4–6px: controls use `rounded-sm`, containers
    `rounded-md`. The only pill shapes are where the shape is the meaning (progress track, switch
    track, radio dot). A rounded corner is never decoration.
12. **Serif for titles only.** `font-serif` on page, card, and dialog headings; never on body copy,
    table cells, form labels, or buttons. The book character lives in the headings alone.
13. **Warm and natural, low saturation.** Warm paper and warm ink; dark mode is warm charcoal, never
    pure black and never cold blue-grey. Status colours stay distinguishable without glowing.

## Components

Primitives live in `frontend/src/components/ui/`. They are shadcn-style: `class-variance-authority`
for variants, Radix primitives where a behaviour is non-trivial, `cn` (`clsx` + `tailwind-merge`)
for class merging.

- Only `Button`, `Alert`, and `Badge` carry status variants. Do not invent colour variants for other
  components.
- `Card` is a paper sheet and carries `paper`. `CardTitle` is `font-serif`; `CardHeader` closes with
  a `rule-ticks-quiet` hairline so the title block reads as a printed form head.
- `Badge` is a stamp: hairline, tracked caps, tinted. It names its status in words. `primary` is the
  one stamp in brand ink; use it for the single state worth marking strongly on a screen.
- `Alert` is a margin note: a coloured left rule and a tinted wash, never a full coloured block.
  `error` uses `role="alert"`.
- `Separator` draws the ruled line. It is the standard section divider.
- `Input`, `Textarea`, `Select`, and `SearchInput` are flat with a hairline that turns `--primary`
  on focus and darkens on hover. `Select` stays a native `<select>` on purpose: the platform picker
  is the control archers know and it survives gloves and one-handed use. Its chevron is a mask in
  `currentColor`, so it flips with the theme.
- `Checkbox` is a square with `checkbox-tick`: green fill and a paper-coloured tick, both from
  tokens. `Switch` and the radio dot are the only pills, because there the shape is the meaning.
- `Table` is a ruled ledger: hairlines between rows, no zebra banding, `tabular-nums` and
  right-aligned figures. `TableKeyValue` renders the label · dotted-leader · value pairs the
  logbook uses for metrics.
- `Progress` is a measured band with fifth-ticks on its track; it keeps `rounded-sm`, since a
  progress track's roundness would mean nothing.
- `Slider` is a distance input drawn as a ruled measure: a compact squared knob on a divided track,
  with a 44px hit area around the knob. Do not enlarge the visible knob to reach the touch minimum.
- `CheckpointSlider` is the same distance input when the Pflöcke are known: the track is divided
  into the coloured bands between them. **The Pflock colours are master data** supplied by the
  Parcours/Ziel data and arrive as a `checkpoints` prop; the design system owns no Pflock palette
  and must never add one to `globals.css`. The value moves freely and never snaps to a stake. Bands
  are `aria-hidden`; the legend repeats every stake with its name, and the thumb's `aria-valuetext`
  names the nearest one. With no checkpoints it renders a plain track.
- `Table` rows that drag are sortable through `@dnd-kit`. The drag activator is a dedicated handle,
  never the whole row; its hit area follows the touch rule. A handle's accessible name must be
  distinct per row and must not invent a position that does not exist (an unshot Pfeil is "nicht
  geschossener Pfeil", not "Pfeil 0"). Numbers in tables are `tabular-nums` and right-aligned;
  machine values are mono. Table heads are tracked small caps on `--surface-muted`.
- `Field` owns label, hint, and error placement. Use it instead of hand-assembling label + input.
- Layout components (`app-shell`, `sidebar`, `page-chrome`) and workflow components are composed
  from the primitives. The active nav item is marked by the waymark triangle plus `aria-current`,
  never by colour alone.

## Screen composition

A screen is a page of the logbook. **The rules below govern appearance only.** How a journey is
structured — whether a parcours is shown as a map, a progress bar, or a plain step list — is a
product decision and is not settled by this identity.

1. The page header is a serif title with a hairline ruled line beneath it.
2. Content blocks are `Card`s (sheets) or ruled table sections; between sections sits a `Separator`.
3. Work is dense and scannable: one primary action, tables for records, forms stacked in `Field`.
4. Numbers in rows: right-aligned, `tabular-nums`. IDs, references, coordinates: `--font-mono`.
5. Exactly one primary action per screen, in the first action row. Everything else secondary/ghost.
6. Status always as word (+ icon or stamp), colour only as second channel.

## The gallery at `/dev`

`frontend/src/app/dev/page.dev.tsx` exists only under `next dev`: `next.config.ts` adds the
`dev.tsx` / `dev.ts` page extensions only in the development phase, so `next build` never emits the
route. `build:frontend` asserts that `out/dev` and the gallery code are absent from the export.

- The page is laid out as a printed specimen book: a cover title block, a table-of-contents index,
  then numbered plates. Each `ComponentSection` carries a two-digit `index` in mono down the
  margin, a serif title, a one-line rationale, and a ruled line before the specimens.
- Every plate renders its content twice — on `.light` (Papier) and `.dark` (Lampe) — so a palette
  change is judged side by side instead of from memory.
- The Tokens plate reads the computed CSS variables and prints the contrast ratio for every
  documented pair, with an AA pass/fail stamp.
- The touch-target toggle outlines interactive elements to reveal targets under 44px. The
  outlines are dashed amber on purpose: they are a measuring overlay, not part of the identity.
- The Datenbilder plate shows the drawing vocabulary as elements — target face, measurement
  series on graph paper, ruled scale, stamp legend — deliberately without screens or flows.
- Earlier identity proposals have been removed. The gallery shows the accepted system only; a
  superseded direction belongs in the spec's decision log, not on the page.

Workflow for a visual change: edit `globals.css` or a component, look at `/dev`, decide, then write
the result into this rule and the spec decision log **in the same commit**.

## Keeping this rule in sync

- A new or renamed token, a changed pairing, a new component variant, or a changed visual rule must
  be reflected in this file and in `specs/product/visual-identity.md` in the same commit. There is
  no "document it later".
- `check:design` (part of `mise run check`) fails when a token exists in `globals.css` but is not
  documented here, or when a raw palette class appears under `frontend/src` outside `globals.css`.
- Prose nuance cannot be checked mechanically. The PR checklist item "design rule and spec
  updated" is the human half of the same obligation.
